<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="dimension-4" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Dimension 4: Access and Relevance</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      This dimension entails setting learning activities in contexts which are relevant and
      meaningful to students, using (formal and informal) language familiar with students, and
      adopting multiple modalities (manipulatives, diagrams, tables, pictures, and more abstract
      representations) to help students advance their understanding. We aim for students to be
      actively engaged using different modalities and gain ownership of them.
    </p>

    <p>
      A key part of making relevant and engaging activities is to make the content relatable to
      the students. One aspect of this is applicable to mathematics as a whole, that there needs
      to be a visible connection between the abstract math concepts and the everyday reality of
      students. This is important for students to be able to build upon their own formal and
      informal learned experiences (funds of knowledge).
    </p>

    <p>
      An important and critical aspect is making sure that the math situations make sense to each
      individual student. This means that mathematical interactions should be tailored to the
      knowledge and interests of the student. What makes sense to one student may not be useful
      to another student so mentors must be prepared with multiple ways to introduce a problem,
      as well as multiple tools that can lead them to understand it and, ultimately, solve it.
      This includes using a large range of concepts, representations, and interactive components,
      and knowing how to move between them, to make visible connections (for example, moving from
      diagram to table, or from certain spatial manipulative to writing numerical expressions).
    </p>

    <p>
      In many activities one objective is to introduce students to certain mathematical terms, and
      teach them to reason based on those terms. The natural inclination is then to push students
      to use this terminology when thinking about a problem and to be precise in their work, but
      this can often be counterproductive. Just as we need to connect concepts to real world
      situations for them to be relevant to students, using common language the students are
      comfortable with is important to their understanding. After establishing their understanding
      of the concepts they can translate to more formal language, gradually. Language can also be
      an integral part of cultural identity, so allowing students to speak other languages or use
      slang makes the content more relatable and shows that they are a valued part of the program
      in whatever way they choose to participate. <q>Slang is not an obstacle for understanding,
      but rather an advantage!</q>
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="dim4-beliefs">
    <title>Mentors' Beliefs and Attitudes</title>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>I believe that learning is more meaningful when students connect to their experiences.</li>
        <li>I believe that students can understand complex ideas if explained clearly.</li>
        <li>I believe that curiosity is a powerful motivator to introduce tasks.</li>
        <li>I believe that multiple ways of representing ideas help students discover and understand concepts deeply.</li>
        <li>I believe that informal language opens doors to learning before introducing more formal academic terms.</li>
        <li>I believe that students' voices, experiences, and examples enrich learning activities.</li>
        <li>I believe that flexible tools and choices allow students to express their understanding creatively.</li>
        <li>I believe that struggles are opportunities to give new explanations and build deeper comprehension.</li>
        <li>I believe that humor, surprises, and unusual examples can ignite curiosity and make learning more memorable.</li>
        <li>I believe that celebrating diverse ways of thinking and representing ideas encourages confidence and engagement.</li>
        <li>I believe that I should help students see why what they are learning matters.</li>
      </ul>
    </p>

    <p>Which other beliefs can shape your practice towards relevance, language, and representations?</p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="dim4-components">
    <title>Components</title>

    <subsubsection xml:id="dim4-meaningful-tasks">
      <title>Component 1: Meaningful Tasks</title>
      <p>Include activities that connect to students' cultures and lives, and spark curiosity.</p>

      <paragraphs>
        <title>Mentors' Actions</title>
        <p>
          <ul>
            <li>Introduce scenarios and stories that are relevant and exciting for students.</li>
            <li>Show how concepts can relate to students' everyday lives.</li>
            <li>Use analogies, humor, unusual visuals, or surprising controversies to spark curiosity.</li>
            <li>Offer different ways for students to approach and make sense of the content.</li>
            <li>Highlight a <q>why this matters</q> for each task, helping students see its purpose.</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </paragraphs>

      <paragraphs>
        <title>Students' Perspectives</title>
        <p>
          <ul>
            <li><q>I see why this topic matters.</q></li>
            <li><q>I like when the examples are about stuff I do.</q></li>
            <li><q>It's fun when I hear stories I can relate to.</q></li>
            <li><q>I can see how this has to do with real life.</q></li>
            <li><q>I like when I can give my own example.</q></li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </paragraphs>
    </subsubsection>

    <subsubsection xml:id="dim4-accessible-language">
      <title>Component 2: Accessible Language</title>
      <p>Explain clearly, and support diverse language and communication.</p>

      <paragraphs>
        <title>Mentors' Actions</title>
        <p>
          <ul>
            <li>(Planning) Use clear, simple words and sentences to explain complex ideas.</li>
            <li>Encourage starting with informal language and gently and gradually connect it to more formal terms.</li>
            <li>Respect and cherish the use of different communication styles, including multiple languages, idioms and cultural gestures.</li>
            <li>Rephrase ideas if needed, and encourage students to explain concepts in their own words.</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </paragraphs>

      <paragraphs>
        <title>Students' Perspectives</title>
        <p>
          <ul>
            <li><q>I can talk in the way I normally speak.</q></li>
            <li><q>I like that my mentor doesn't just use confusing words without explaining.</q></li>
            <li><q>I like that my mentor really tries to make sure that we understand things.</q></li>
            <li><q>I feel okay asking questions because I can use my own words.</q></li>
            <li><q>I can explain stuff in my own way and they get it.</q></li>
            <li><q>I like that we can mix Spanish and English if we need to.</q></li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </paragraphs>
    </subsubsection>

    <subsubsection xml:id="dim4-representations">
      <title>Component 3: Powerful Representations</title>
      <p>Use manipulatives, visuals, and models effectively to build understanding.</p>

      <paragraphs>
        <title>Mentors' Actions</title>
        <p>
          <ul>
            <li>Introduce and show examples of representations, and explicitly guide students in understanding how they work.</li>
            <li>Help students explicitly relate different representations to understand things deeper.</li>
            <li>(Planning) Consider the pros and cons of each representation that you plan to use, and useful progressions to use them.</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </paragraphs>

      <paragraphs>
        <title>Students' Perspectives</title>
        <p>
          <ul>
            <li><q>I understand things more when I see pictures or graphs.</q></li>
            <li><q>I like it when we use tables, drawings, or real objects.</q></li>
            <li><q>Seeing different ways of showing the problem helps me get it.</q></li>
            <li><q>I like when the mentor shows how two diagrams can show the same data.</q></li>
            <li><q>I feel like I can make more sense when I see examples.</q></li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </paragraphs>
    </subsubsection>

    <subsubsection xml:id="dim4-modalities">
      <title>Component 4: Empower to Use Modalities Flexibly</title>
      <p>Help students choose and adapt ways to represent ideas and systems.</p>

      <paragraphs>
        <title>Mentors' Actions</title>
        <p>
          <ul>
            <li>Give differentiated scaffolds using words, visuals, or other representations.</li>
            <li>Let students pick or adapt representations to express ideas and complete tasks.</li>
            <li>Allow students to explain to each other using their own language and representations.</li>
            <li>(Planning) Have alternative explanations and representations ready for students who need extra help.</li>
            <li>Celebrate creative or unusual representations that students invent.</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </paragraphs>

      <paragraphs>
        <title>Students' Perspectives</title>
        <p>
          <ul>
            <li><q>Sometimes, I can solve problems in the way I want.</q></li>
            <li><q>I can pick the tools or drawings that make sense to me.</q></li>
            <li><q>I feel okay trying my own way, even if it's different.</q></li>
            <li><q>If I get stuck, my mentor gives me new options to see what's going on.</q></li>
            <li><q>I like that my mentor lets me explain with my own method.</q></li>
            <li><q>I feel supported when I use my own strategies or models.</q></li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </paragraphs>
    </subsubsection>

  </subsection>

</section>
